# PyQuest infrastructure configuration — spec §6.1
#
#   cp .env.example .env    then edit .env and replace every CHANGEME.
#
# .env is gitignored at the repo root. .env.example is committed and must never
# contain a real secret. The values below are placeholders, not defaults you can
# ship: the stack refuses to start if a required variable is missing, but it will
# happily start with these, and then a learner's git remote has a password anyone
# who has read this repository knows.

# =============================================================================
# Postgres — one server, two databases (§6.1)
# =============================================================================

# Superuser role and the pyquest progress database (§6.7: progress lives here,
# content lives in git, and the two never mix).
POSTGRES_USER=pyquest
POSTGRES_PASSWORD=CHANGEME-postgres-superuser-password
POSTGRES_DB=pyquest

# Host port for Postgres. Bound to 127.0.0.1 only — see docker-compose.yml.
# 5432 is deliberately NOT the default: this machine already runs an unrelated
# Postgres on 5432. Change this only if 5433 is also taken.
POSTGRES_PORT=5433

# =============================================================================
# Gitea's database, inside that same Postgres server
# =============================================================================
# Created on FIRST BOOT ONLY by initdb/01-create-gitea-db.sh. Changing these
# after the volume exists does not migrate anything — see README, "Changing the
# Gitea database credentials".
GITEA_DB_NAME=gitea
GITEA_DB_USER=gitea
GITEA_DB_PASSWORD=CHANGEME-gitea-database-password

# =============================================================================
# Gitea — the self-hosted git remote (§6.5)
# =============================================================================

# Signs sessions and encrypts stored tokens. Generate a fresh one:
#   docker run --rm gitea/gitea:1.27.2 gitea generate secret SECRET_KEY
# Changing it later invalidates every session and every stored OAuth token.
GITEA_SECRET_KEY=CHANGEME-run-the-generate-command-above

# Host ports for Gitea. Unlike Postgres these bind on ALL interfaces, because
# §6.4 makes `git push` the verification mechanism and learners push from their
# own laptop over the LAN.
GITEA_HTTP_PORT=3080
GITEA_SSH_PORT=3022

# The hostname another learner's machine will use to reach this one. `localhost`
# works for a browser on this host and for the smoke test, but nobody else can push to
# it. Set this to this machine's LAN name or IP before handing out the remote —
# it is baked into the clone URLs Gitea displays.
#   e.g. GITEA_DOMAIN=parent-desktop.local
GITEA_DOMAIN=localhost
GITEA_ROOT_URL=http://localhost:3080/

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# What the api needs to read a learner's repository
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WITHOUT THESE TWO, `local-repo` AND `git-signal` REFUSE EVERY SUBMISSION.
# That is 10 of the 30 authored quests. The api says so at boot — "no
# GITEA_TOKEN: local-repo and git-signal will refuse rather than guess" — and
# then serves a campaign a third of which cannot be verified.
#
# A Gitea access token for whoever holds the DM seat. Mint one on this machine:
#   cd infra && docker compose exec -T --user git gitea \
#     gitea admin user generate-access-token \
#       --username <dm> --token-name pyquest --scopes write:repository --raw
#
# `write:repository` rather than read: the same token is what
# `tools/learner-setup/pack.sh --remote` pushes the setup branch with.
GITEA_TOKEN=

# Which repository belongs to which player, as `handle=owner/name`, separated by
# commas or semicolons. The handle is the PyQuest one; the owner is the Gitea
# account, and §7 lets the learner choose the repository name — so the two halves
# genuinely differ and this is the mapping between them.
#   e.g. PLAYER_REPOS=steve=steve/journal, kid=kid/quests
#
# This belongs in a `players` column and is an env var only until the db track
# adds one. `apps/api/src/gitea.ts` says the same thing at the parser.
PLAYER_REPOS=

# =============================================================================
# Backups — §6.9
# =============================================================================
# Nightly pg_dump plus a mirror of every Gitea repository, dated tarball,
# thirty-day retention.
#
# POINT THIS AT A SECOND PHYSICAL DISK. A backup on the same disk as the data
# survives `rm -rf` and a bad migration; it does not survive the disk failing,
# which is the failure §6.9 exists to cover. The Journal and a learner's commit
# history are the two artifacts this project cannot regenerate.
#
# Git Bash path syntax: /d/pyquest-backups means D:\pyquest-backups.
# A NAS or external drive is fine. The path is created if it does not exist.
BACKUP_DEST=/c/pyquest-backups

# Days to keep. §6.9 says thirty.
BACKUP_RETENTION_DAYS=30

# =============================================================================
# The dev stack — content and API development (§6.1)
# =============================================================================
# The third way to run the SPA, beside prod and fixtures. `npm run dev` answers
# from apps/web/src/fixtures, which are deliberately sparse and tell you nothing
# about real content; prod serves the real thing but needs an image rebuild for
# an API change. This is the one where a brief you just edited shows up in a
# browser refresh.
#
# It runs the api FROM SOURCE on the host, so both content and API code reload
# without a build. Nothing here stops or re-ports a production container:
# 3081 and 3082 stay bound to the images, because 3082 is the learner's front
# door and taking it down to look at a lesson is not a trade worth making.

# The dev api's port. Its own, not the production api's, and bound to 127.0.0.1
# for the same reason — nothing off this machine dials it. Continues the
# 3080-3082 block; see README, "Ports".
DEV_API_PORT=3083

# A THIRD database on the same Postgres server, beside `pyquest` and `gitea`.
#
# This one is not optional and it is not tidiness. Signing in writes a token row;
# clicking through a quest writes attempts, medals and journal entries. A dev api
# pointed at POSTGRES_DB would do content review by mutating the learner's real
# progress — and §6.7 puts progress in Postgres precisely so it is the thing that
# cannot be regenerated from git.
#
# Created and migrated the same way the first one is. Seed it with a throwaway
# household and click through that instead.
DEV_POSTGRES_DB=pyquest_dev
